##fileformat=VCFv4.3
##INFO=<ID=X,Extra=a<b,Number=1,Type=Integer,Description="d">
##contig=<ID=chr1,length=1000000>
##Authority=VCFv4.3.tex:117 -- "A structured meta-information line is similar, but the value is itself a comma-separated list of key=value pairs, enclosed within `<' and `>' characters" -- is the target spec's whole statement of this grammar: it defines no escaping for a `<` inside an attribute value and forbids none either (the `<`/`>` exclusions at VCFv4.3.tex:200 and :265 are scoped to an ##ALT ID and to contig names respectively, not to a generic attribute value). Silence, not prohibition, so warn-and-preserve is the expected outcome rather than rejection.
##Authority=bcftools 1.23.1 (htslib 1.23.1): an unescaped `<` in an unquoted attribute value increments bcf_hdr_parse_line's own bracket-nesting counter (`~/Dev/htslib/vcf.c:730`, "if ( *q=='<' ) nopen++;"), so bcftools scans past the declaration's own closing `>` and warns "Incomplete header line" while the file still reads at exit 0 -- measured live and pinned by BcftoolsDependencyTest#testUnescapedLessThanInMetadataValueBcftoolsMisparsesButSurvives, and tabled with its own citations as STRUCTURAL_DELIMITER_HANDLING.md's Row 4. That a real tool garbles this byte sequence is exactly what the warning this fixture pins tells a caller.
##Authority=Already-documented parse-time behavior, not a new discovery: STRUCTURAL_DELIMITER_HANDLING.md's Row 31 (written 2026-08-15) records the identical warning -- InternalUtils.parseOneAttribute's structural-character warning at InternalUtils.java:504-505 -- as a parse-time outcome, for an unquoted value ending in a quote. This fixture pins the same warning for the `<` trigger of the same check. Note that VCF_SPEC_AMBIGUITIES.md's "Unquoted metadata attribute value: malformed bracket vs. `<`/`>`/`;`" entry describes a SEPARATE, EncodingProblem-based check (throws at construction, warns at validateBeforeWrite()), which is NOT what fires here; that entry's prose omitting the parse-time warning is filed in TODO.md. This fixture has no comma at all in the Extra value, so there is nothing for the attribute-list splitter to truncate on, but the value is still flagged (not silently preserved) because it contains a raw `<`.
##ExpectWarnContains=containing a character bcftools treats as structural; preserving it
#CHROM	POS	ID	REF	ALT	QUAL	FILTER	INFO
chr1	1	rs1	A	G	.	PASS	X=abc
